essing the laboratory-based evaluation with monitoring. I've noted protocols for maximal exercise testing vary in intensity and rate. I'll include the 6 Minute Walk Test definition, procedure, and clinical significance for the assessment.\n\n\n**Detailing Exercise Protocols**\n\nI'm now detailing the Modified Bruce Protocol. I've defined its theoretical basis, which accounts for cardiac patients. Then I am writing the protocol steps, focusing on the additional lower-intensity stages and clinical suitability. I'll maintain the block format, with only allowed punctuation.\n\n\n**Analyzing Protocol Parameters**\n\nI'm now analyzing the Balke protocol's parameters, focusing on its theoretical basis, procedure (constant speed, elevation increases), and clinical application for specific patient populations. I've updated the reference format. I'm adding a specific point on cardiovascular adaptation and limitations for context.\n\n\n**Refining Borg Scale Details**\n\nI'm now detailing the Borg RPE scale, refining details on both original and modified versions to fit the strict punctuation rules This encompasses defining rating ranges, theoretical basis, clinical significance, and sensitivity to dyspnea and leg fatigue all within the continuous text block.\n\n\n**Expanding Borg Scale Content**\n\nI am now detailing the Borg scale's specifics. I'm focusing on rating ranges, theoretical basis, clinical significance, including medications, and added sensitivity to dyspnea and leg fatigue, within a continuous block. I'm focusing on adhering to punctuation restrictions.\n\n\n**Defining Dyspnea & NYHA**\n\nI'm now detailing both the mMRC and NYHA scales in a punctuation-limited continuous block. I am focused on mMRC grades 0-4, noting corresponding activity limitations, before moving to detail the NYHA Class II.\n\n\n**Defining NYHA Class IV**\n\nI am now detailing the New York Heart Association (NYHA) Class IV definition in a continuous, punctuation-limited block, including how p