{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T14:22:14Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nSubjective Examination: Demographic Data. Chief complaint, History of Present illness including functional limitation, Past Medical Surgical history, Family history, Occupational & Personal history, Socio-Economic, Environmental and Psychological History, Drug and Treatment History///// musculoskeletal —-Vital Parameters\r\n Temperature: State route used (axillary/oral) and presence of fever.\r\n Normal Value: 98.6°F (37°C) (Range: 97.8°F – 99.1°F / 36.5°C – 37.3°C)\r\n Respiratory Rate (RR): Count rate, rhythm, depth, and pattern (thoraco-abdominal in females, abdomino-thoracic in males).\r\n Normal Value: 12–20 breaths/min, regular, quiet, and unlabored.\r\n Peripheral Pulses: Palpate radial, dorsalis pedis, or posterior tibial; document rate, rhythm, volume, and bilateral symmetry.\r\n Normal Value: 60–100 beats/min, regular, Grade 2+ (normal, palpable bilaterally).\r\n Blood Pressure (BP): Record systolic/diastolic pressure, patient position (sitting/supine), and arm used.\r\n Normal Value: <120/80 mmHg (Systolic: 100–120 mmHg, Diastolic: 60–80 mmHg).\r\nGeneral Musculoskeletal Observations\r\n Body Built: Document somatotype (ectomorphic, mesomorphic, endomorphic) and calculated BMI.\r\n Normal Finding: Mesomorphic build; BMI 18.5–24.9 kg/m².\r\n Attitude of Limb: Note resting spatial position of the limb relative to anatomical neutral (e.g., antalgic posturing, guarding).\r\n Normal Finding: Symmetrical, resting comfortably in anatomical neutral without protective spasm.\r\n Bony Contours: Inspect continuity, prominence, and bilateral symmetry of standard bony landmarks.\r\n Normal Finding: Intact, smooth, and symmetrical, without step-offs, spurs, or abnormal prominence.\r\n Deformities: Identify structural (fixed) vs. postural (flexible) deformities, plane of deviation, and joint involved.\r\n Normal Finding: Absent; normal physiological axial and appendicular alignment.\r\n Trophic Changes: Assess skin texture, shine, hair loss, nail changes, pigmentation, or signs of autonomic dysfunction.\r\n Normal Finding: Warm, supple skin with normal turgor, uniform hair distribution, and smooth nails; no trophic ulcers, glossiness, or cyanosis.\r\nExamination of Posture\r\n Postural Alignment: Observe alignment relative to a standard plumb line across anterior, lateral, and posterior views:\r\n Anterior: Head level, shoulder symmetry, pelvic level (ASIS), Q-angle, patellar alignment, arches of feet.\r\n Lateral: Alignment through earlobe \\rightarrow acromion \\rightarrow lumbar bodies \\rightarrow greater trochanter \\rightarrow anterior knee \\rightarrow anterior lateral malleolus.\r\n Posterior: Scapular level, vertical spinal line, PSIS symmetry, popliteal folds, calcaneal alignment.\r\n Normal Finding: Balanced, symmetrical posture; alignment matches plumb line reference points; normal physiological curves (cervical lordosis, thoracic kyphosis, lumbar lordosis) with neutral pelvic tilt (7^\\circ\\text{--}15^\\circ).\r\nPalpation & Soft Tissue Examination\r\n Muscle Spasm: Palpate muscle belly for involuntary sustained contraction, taut bands, and resistance to stretch.\r\n Normal Finding: Normal resting muscle tone; tissue is soft, pliable, and non-reactive to passive elongation.\r\n Tenderness: Localize exact anatomical structure and grade using the Ritchie scale:\r\n Grade 0: No tenderness.\r\n Grade I: Complaint of pain without facial grimace.\r\n Grade II: Complaint of pain with facial grimace/wince.\r\n Grade III: Severe pain causing withdrawal of the body part.\r\n Grade IV: Patient resists or prevents palpation.\r\n Normal Finding: Grade 0 (non-tender to firm palpation).\r\n Edema / Swelling: Document intra-articular effusion vs. extra-articular edema; test for pitting (1+ to 4+ scale) and localized warmth.\r\n Normal Finding: Absent / Grade 0; no localized heat, pitting, or fluid fluctuation.\r\n Limb Girth / Muscle Wasting: Measure circumference in centimeters using a non-elastic tape at set distances from fixed bony landmarks (e.g., 5 cm, 10 cm, 15 cm from superior pole of patella or medial epicondyle). Compare bilaterally.\r\n Normal Finding: Bilateral symmetry (a difference of \\le 0.5\\text{--}1\\text{ cm} on the dominant side is acceptable as physiological).\r\n Scar Examination: Note exact anatomical site, size (L \\times W in cm), type (surgical, traumatic, hypertrophic, keloid), healing stage, mobility over underlying planes, and adherence.\r\n Normal Finding: If present post-operatively: mature, pale, flat, pliable, fully mobile over fascia, and non-tender. /////Range of Motion (ROM)\r\n What to Write: Document Active ROM (AROM) and Passive ROM (PROM) using a universal goniometer; note plane, degrees of excursion, presence of a painful arc, and compensatory movement patterns.\r\n Normal Finding: Full, pain-free physiological range matching standard anatomical norms without substitution; AROM equals PROM (e.g., Shoulder Flexion 0\\text{--}180^\\circ, Knee Flexion 0\\text{--}135^\\circ/140^\\circ).\r\nJoint Play & End Feel\r\n What to Write:\r\n Joint Play (Accessory Motion): Evaluate passive glide, distraction, roll, and spin in the loose-packed position (graded as normal, hypomobile, or hypermobile).\r\n End Feel (Cyriax): Document sensation at extreme passive limit (Soft, Firm, Hard) or pathological end-feel (Empty, Springy block, Boggy/Spasm).\r\n Normal Finding: Normal accessory excursion with painless gliding; physiological end feel appropriate for the joint tested:\r\n Soft (Tissue approximation): Knee flexion, elbow flexion.\r\n Firm (Capsular/Ligamentous): Shoulder external rotation, ankle dorsiflexion.\r\n Hard (Bony contact): Elbow extension.\r\nMuscle Strength (Manual Muscle Testing - MMT)\r\n What to Write: Rate isolated muscle groups using the Daniels & Worthingham / MRC scale (Grade 0 to Grade 5); specify anti-gravity vs. gravity-eliminated test positions and response to the \"break test.\"\r\n Normal Finding: Grade 5 (Normal) — completes full active ROM against gravity and tolerates maximum manual resistance without breaking.\r\nMuscle Power\r\n What to Write: Rate the ability to generate force rapidly (\\text{Force} \\times \\text{Velocity}); document functional explosive tests (e.g., vertical jump height, standing broad jump, medicine ball toss) or isokinetic dynamometry.\r\n Normal Finding: Symmetrical, high-velocity force generation; Limb Symmetry Index (LSI) \\ge 90\\text{--}100\\% compared to the contralateral side; within age/sex normative percentiles.\r\nMuscle Endurance\r\n What to Write: Assess the capacity of a muscle group to sustain repetitive contractions or hold a static posture over time (e.g., timed static plank, Biering-Sørensen test for trunk extensors, dynamic repetitions to fatigue at 60–70% 1RM).\r\n Normal Finding: Symmetrical holding capacity without compensatory tremors (e.g., Biering-Sørensen test sustained for >60\\text{--}120\\text{ seconds}).\r\nMuscle Flexibility (Muscle Length Testing)\r\n What to Write: Record specific length assessment tests for 2-joint and postural muscles (e.g., Thomas test for iliopsoas, Ober’s test for ITB/TFL, Ely’s test for rectus femoris, 90-90 Straight Leg Raise for hamstrings).\r\n Normal Finding: Normal physiological muscle length without restricted joint range or compensatory substitution:\r\n Thomas Test: Thigh remains flat on table with knee flexed to \\ge 80^\\circ.\r\n 90-90 SLR: Knee extends to within 10^\\circ\\text{--}20^\\circ of full extension.\r\n Ober's Test: Thigh drops below the horizontal plane.\r\nIsometric Resisted Testing (Cyriax Method)\r\n What to Write: Test muscle groups in a loose-packed/neutral joint position to isolate contractile elements; record pain and strength response using the four Cyriax categories:\r\n Strong and Painless (SPL)\r\n Strong and Painful (SPF)\r\n Weak and Painful (WPF)\r\n Weak and Painless (WPL)\r\n Normal Finding: Strong and Painless (SPL) — signifies intact, uninjured contractile tissue and normal neural conduction.\r\nLimb-Length Discrepancy (LLD)\r\n What to Write: Measure both lower extremities in supine using a non-elastic tape measure:\r\n True (Anatomic) Length: Anterior Superior Iliac Spine (ASIS) to Medial Malleolus (clearing medial knee joint line).\r\n Apparent (Functional) Length: Xiphisternum or Umbilicus to Medial Malleolus.\r\n Normal Finding: Equal bilaterally; True LLD <1\\text{ cm} (<0.5\\text{ inches}) is considered clinically insignificant/physiological.\r\nPelvic Inclination (Pelvic Tilt)\r\n What to Write: Palpate and measure the sagittal alignment between the ASIS and Posterior Superior Iliac Spine (PSIS) in comfortable bipedal stance using an inclinometer.\r\n Normal Finding: Normal anterior pelvic tilt of 7^\\circ\\text{--}15^\\circ (ASIS lies approximately 0.5\\text{--}1\\text{ cm} inferior to PSIS in horizontal plane); transverse level symmetrical bilaterally.\r\nExamination of Balance\r\n What to Write: Document static and dynamic postural control:\r\n Static Balance: Single-Leg Stance (SLS) with eyes open/closed, Romberg and Sharpened Romberg tests.\r\n Dynamic Balance: Functional Reach Test (FRT), Timed Up and Go (TUG), Y-Balance Test.\r\n Normal Finding:\r\n Single-Leg Stance: Sustained for >30\\text{ seconds} (eyes open), >10\\text{--}20\\text{ seconds} (eyes closed).\r\n Romberg Test: Negative (steady posture maintained for 30 seconds with eyes closed; minimal sway).\r\n Functional Reach Test: Reach distance >25\\text{ cm} (>10\\text{ inches}).\r\n Timed Up and Go (TUG): Completed in <10\\text{ seconds}.\r\nGait Examination\r\n What to Write: Analyze stance phase (heel strike, foot flat, midstance, push-off) and swing phase (acceleration, midswing, deceleration); record cadence, step length, stride width, and check for pathological deviations (antalgic, Trendelenburg, high-steppage, circumduction).\r\n Normal Finding: Normal, symmetrical, smooth gait pattern:\r\n Cadence: 100–120 steps/min.\r\n Base of Support (Step Width): 5–10 cm.\r\n Stance-to-Swing Ratio: 60% stance, 40% swing.\r\n Equal stride length bilaterally, normal pelvis/trunk counter-rotation, and reciprocal arm swing.\r\nExternal Aids & Assistive Devices\r\n What to Write: Record the presence and type of mobility device (standard walker, axillary/elbow crutches, quad cane, AFO, knee brace), prescribed weight-bearing status (NWB, PWB, TTWB, FWB), gait sequence used (2-point, 3-point, 4-point), and degree of user independence.\r\n Normal Finding: Independent community ambulator; no external walking aids, orthoses, or bracing required.///// Superficial Sensation (Touch, Pain, Temp):\r\n Test: Cotton wisp (light touch), pinprick (pain), hot/cold tubes (temperature) bilaterally.\r\n Normal: Intact bilaterally; prompt and accurate localization.\r\n Deep Sensation (Proprioception, Kinesthesia, Vibration):\r\n Test: Passive digit displacement (joint position/kinesthesia), 128 Hz tuning fork on bony prominences (vibration).\r\n Normal: Intact bilaterally; detects minor joint excursion (<1^\\circ\\text{--}2^\\circ); symmetrical vibration perception.\r\n Cortical Sensation (Stereognosis, 2PD, Graphesthesia):\r\n Test: Object identification by touch, 2-point discrimination on fingertips, tracing numbers on palm.\r\n Normal: Intact bilaterally; 2-point discrimination <4\\text{--}5\\text{ mm} on fingertips.\r\n Dermatomes (Sensory Key Points):\r\n Test: Light touch and pinprick across standard ASIA levels (C2–T1 upper quadrant; L1–S5 lower quadrant).\r\n Normal: Score 2/2 (Intact bilaterally) across all levels; no sensory loss or hyperalgesia.\r\n Myotomes (Motor Root Screen):\r\n Test: 5-second isometric hold (C1–C4 neck/scapula, C5 shoulder, C6 elbow flex/wrist ext, C7 elbow ext/wrist flex, C8 thumb/fingers, T1 intrinsics; L1–L2 hip flex, L3 knee ext, L4 dorsiflex, L5 EHL, S1 plantarflex).\r\n Normal: Grade 5/5 bilaterally; strong and non-fatiguing.\r\n Deep Tendon Reflexes (DTRs):\r\n Test: Biceps (C5–C6), Brachioradialis (C5–C6), Triceps (C7–C8), Knee (L3–L4), Ankle (S1–S2) on a 0–4+ scale.\r\n Normal: Grade 2+ bilaterally; symmetrical, brisk response.\r\n Superficial Reflexes:\r\n Test: Plantar stroke (S1–S2), abdominal quadrant strokes (T7–T12).\r\n Normal: Plantar is Flexor (toes curl down); abdominal reflex is present and symmetrical.\r\n Pathological Reflexes (UMN Signs):\r\n Test: Babinski response, Hoffmann’s sign (middle finger flick), Ankle clonus (rapid dorsiflexion).\r\n Normal: Negative / Absent bilaterally; clonus absent (<2 beats).\r\n////// Power Grips:\r\n Assessment: Test ability to form and maintain standard functional grasp patterns:\r\n Cylindrical Grip: Holding a bottle or cylinder (flexor digitorum superficialis/profundus, thenar/hypothenar lock).\r\n Spherical Grip: Holding a ball with digits abducted and spread (intrinsics plus long flexors).\r\n Hook Grip: Carrying a handle or bag (IP flexion without thumb opposition; relies purely on long digital flexors).\r\n Fist / Grasp: Clenching tightly around an object (requires 20^\\circ\\text{--}30^\\circ wrist extension).\r\n Grip Strength Measurement: Jamar Hand Dynamometer (tested seated, shoulder adducted, elbow at 90^\\circ, wrist in neutral to 30^\\circ extension; average of 3 trials).\r\n Normal Value: Symmetrical, complete grip closure; Jamar values: 30–50 kg (males), 15–30 kg (females); dominant hand is typically \\sim 10\\% stronger than non-dominant.\r\n Precision Grips (Hand Pinches):\r\n Assessment: Test prehension patterns and measure force using a hydraulic pinch gauge:\r\n Tip-to-Tip (Terminal / Pincer) Pinch: Index fingertip to thumb tip (picking up a needle/pin; tests Anterior Interosseous Nerve / FDP & FPL).\r\n Pad-to-Pad (Palmar / 3-Jaw Chuck) Pinch: Thumb pad opposed to index and middle finger pads (holding a pencil or coin).\r\n Lateral (Key) Pinch: Thumb pad pressed against the lateral/radial aspect of the index middle phalanx (turning a key; tests Adductor Pollicis / Ulnar Nerve).\r\n Normal Value: Clean opposition without collapse of interphalangeal joints; Pinch gauge averages:\r\n Key Pinch: 7–10 kg\r\n Palmar (3-Jaw) Pinch: 5–8 kg\r\n Tip Pinch: 4–6 kg\r\n Routine Hand Motor Evaluation:\r\n Thenar Muscles (Median Nerve): Test Abductor Pollicis Brevis (thumb abduction perpendicular to palm) and Opponens Pollicis (thumb pulp to little finger base).\r\n Hypothenar & Intrinsics (Ulnar Nerve): Test Dorsal Interossei (finger abduction against resistance), Palmar Interossei (finger adduction/card test), and Lumbricals (MCP flexion with IP extension).\r\n Special Motor Signs:\r\n Froment's Sign: Pull paper from between thumb and index finger; assesses Adductor Pollicis.\r\n Normal Finding: Negative (thumb IP remains extended; no compensatory FPL hyperflexion).\r\n Normal Motor Finding: Grade 5/5 bilaterally on all intrinsic/extrinsic tests; complete thumb opposition (Kapandji score: 10/10).\r\n Routine Hand Sensory Evaluation:\r\n Autonomous Sensory Zones:\r\n Median Nerve: Volar pulp of the index finger.\r\n Ulnar Nerve: Volar pulp of the little finger.\r\n Radial Nerve: Dorsal aspect of the first web space.\r\n Static Two-Point Discrimination (Weber Test): Apply blunt prongs parallel to finger axis on the distal digital pulp with eyes closed.\r\n Normal Value: \\le 5\\text{ mm} (classified as Normal static 2PD).\r\n Moving Two-Point Discrimination (Dellon Test): Move prongs longitudinally from proximal to distal along the finger pad.\r\n Normal Value: 2\\text{--}3\\text{ mm}.\r\n Semmes-Weinstein Monofilament (Pressure Threshold): Apply monofilament perpendicular to skin until it bows for 1.5 seconds.\r\n Normal Value: 2.83 monofilament (0.07 g) indicates intact normal light touch.\r\n Stereognosis & Functional Hand Dexterity:\r\n Tactile Stereognosis: Place 5–6 common objects (key, coin, safety pin, paperclip, cotton ball) into the patient's hand one by one with eyes closed; ask for identification.\r\n Normal Finding: Immediate, accurate recognition within 2–3 seconds per object without vision.\r\n Functional Dexterity Tests:\r\n Moberg Pick-Up Test: Patient picks up small items and places them into a container (timed with eyes open, then repeated with eyes closed).\r\n Nine-Hole Peg Test (NHPT): Place and remove nine small pegs from holes as quickly as possible.\r\n Normal Value: Completed smoothly in <18\\text{--}20\\text{ seconds} with no drops or fumbling; bilateral performance within 10% symmetry.\r\n/////////////Cardiovascular///// Vital Parameters:\r\n Temp: 98.6°F (37°C); afebrile.\r\n RR: 12–20 breaths/min; regular, quiet, unlabored.\r\n Pulse: 60–100 bpm; regular, Grade 2+ volume, bilaterally symmetrical.\r\n BP: <120/80 mmHg; no orthostatic drop.\r\n SpO_2: 95%–100% on room air.\r\n Body Built & General Inspection:\r\n Body Built: Mesomorphic (BMI 18.5–24.9 kg/m²); no cachexia or wasting.\r\n Attitude & Posture: Relaxed sitting/supine; no tripoding or orthopnea.\r\n Bony Contours: Smooth, intact, and symmetrical; no sternal/rib step-offs.\r\n Symmetry & Deformities: Trachea midline; JVP <3\\text{--}4\\text{ cm}; AP:Transverse ratio is 1:2; no clubbing (Schamroth negative).\r\n Trophic Changes: Warm, pink, capillary refill <2\\text{ s}; no cyanosis or pitting edema.\r\n Breathing Pattern & Breath Holding:\r\n Pattern: Eupneic, diaphragmatic-costal; I:E ratio 1:2; no paradoxical motion.\r\n Accessory Muscle Use: Quiescent / absent at rest; no SCM, scalene, or intercostal indrawing.\r\n Breath Holding Test (BHT): Normal holding time >30\\text{--}45\\text{ seconds}.\r\n Cough & Sputum:\r\n Cough: Strong, crisp, effective, and self-clearing.\r\n Sputum: Scant, clear/mucoid, and odorless.\r\n Palpation (Fremitus & Chest Expansion):\r\n Tactile/Vocal Fremitus: Symmetrical, moderate, uniform vibration across all zones.\r\n Chest Expansion: Bilaterally symmetrical excursion; 3\\text{--}5\\text{ cm} difference between full expiration and full inspiration.\r\n Percussion:\r\n Percussion Note: Resonant bilaterally over all lung fields (dullness restricted to normal cardiac and liver borders).\r\n//////Breath Sounds (Lung Auscultation)\r\n Normal Breath Sounds:\r\n Vesicular: Soft, low-pitched, rustling; heard over peripheral lung fields; Inspiration : Expiration = 3:1 (no pause between phases).\r\n Bronchial: Loud, high-pitched, tubular/hollow; heard over trachea/manubrium; Inspiration : Expiration = 1:2 (distinct pause between phases).\r\n Bronchovesicular: Intermediate pitch and intensity; heard over 1st–2nd intercostal spaces anteriorly and interscapular region; Inspiration : Expiration = 1:1.\r\n Normal Finding: Normal vesicular breath sounds heard bilaterally across all lung zones with no added sounds.\r\n Abnormal Breath Sounds:\r\n Bronchial Breathing in Periphery: Pathological; indicates lung consolidation, lobar pneumonia, or large superficial cavity.\r\n Diminished / Absent Sounds: Pleural effusion, pneumothorax, severe atelectasis, or hyperinflated COPD.\r\n Adventitious (Added) Sounds:\r\n Crackles (Rales): Discontinuous, non-musical clicking/popping sounds; fine crackles (pulmonary edema, interstitial fibrosis) vs. coarse crackles (bronchiectasis, chronic bronchitis).\r\n Wheezes (Rhonchi): Continuous, musical, high-pitched whistling (bronchospasm in asthma/COPD) or low-pitched snoring sounds (secretions in large airways).\r\n Pleural Friction Rub: Grating, creaking sound present in both inspiration and expiration (pleurisy/pleuritis).\r\n Stridor: Loud, high-pitched, musical monophonic sound heard predominantly during inspiration; emergency upper airway obstruction.\r\n Vocal Resonance:\r\n Test: Auscultate lung zones while the patient repeats \"ninety-nine\".\r\n Normal: Muffled, indistinct, soft vocal resonance bilaterally.\r\n Abnormal: Bronchophony (words heard loudly and distinctly), Egophony (\"E\" transmits as nasal \"A\" over consolidation), Whispered Pectoriloquy (whispered syllables heard clearly).\r\nHeart Sounds (Cardiac Auscultation)\r\n Normal Heart Sounds:\r\n S1 (\"Lub\"): Closure of Mitral and Tricuspid (AV) valves; marks the onset of ventricular systole; loudest at the apex (5th intercostal space, midclavicular line).\r\n S2 (\"Dub\"): Closure of Aortic and Pulmonary (semilunar) valves; marks the onset of ventricular diastole; loudest at the base (2nd intercostal space, right/left sternal borders).\r\n Normal Finding: S1 and S2 clearly audible, regular rate and rhythm; no extra sounds or murmurs.\r\n Abnormal Heart Sounds:\r\n S3 (Ventricular Gallop): Low-pitched, early diastole (\"Kentucky\" rhythm); pathological in adults >40 years indicating left ventricular failure or fluid overload.\r\n S4 (Atrial Gallop): Low-pitched, late diastole (\"Tennessee\" rhythm); caused by atria contracting against a stiff, non-compliant ventricle (hypertension, LVH).\r\n Murmurs: Turbulent blood flow through stenotic or regurgitant valves; graded Grade 1 (faint) to Grade 6 (audible without stethoscope touching chest).\r\n Pericardial Friction Rub: Superficial, scratchy, high-pitched sound heard best at the left lower sternal border with patient leaning forward (pericarditis).\r\nChest Measurements & Symmetry\r\n Chest Expansion Measurement:\r\n Method: Non-elastic measuring tape used across three levels, measuring the difference from maximal expiration to maximal inspiration:\r\n Axillary Level: 2nd–3rd intercostal space.\r\n Mammary / Nipple Level: 4th intercostal space.\r\n Xiphisternal Level: 8th–10th rib margin / xiphisternal junction.\r\n Normal Value: 3\\text{--}5\\text{ cm} (1.5\\text{--}2\\text{ inches}) difference between full expiration and full inspiration across all levels.\r\n Symmetry of Chest Movement:\r\n Method: Examiner places hands firmly on anterior/posterior chest wall with thumbs pinching a midline skin fold at the upper lobes (clavicular region), middle lobes (axillary/nipple), and lower lobes (10th ribs).\r\n Normal Value: Bilateral, symmetrical, and simultaneous excursion; thumbs diverge equally from the midline by \\sim 2\\text{ cm} on each side during deep inspiration.\r\nAnthropometric Measurements\r\n Body Mass Index (BMI):\r\n Formula: \\text{Weight (kg)} / [\\text{Height (m)}]^2\r\n Classifications (WHO):\r\n Underweight: <18.5\\text{ kg/m}^2\r\n Normal: 18.5\\text{--}24.9\\text{ kg/m}^2\r\n Overweight: 25.0\\text{--}29.9\\text{ kg/m}^2\r\n Obese: \\ge 30.0\\text{ kg/m}^2 (Asian cut-off for obesity: \\ge 25.0\\text{ kg/m}^2)\r\n Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR):\r\n Method: Waist measured at the narrowest circumference between the lower rib margin and iliac crest (umbilicus level); Hip measured at the widest horizontal circumference over the buttocks/greater trochanters.\r\n Formula: \\text{Waist Circumference} / \\text{Hip Circumference}\r\n Normal Values (Low Cardiovascular / Metabolic Risk):\r\n Males: <0.90 (Central obesity: \\ge 0.90)\r\n Females: <0.85 (Central obesity: \\ge 0.85)\r\n////////\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-17T19:52:14+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\n<USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>\nThe user changed setting `Model Selection` from Gemini 3.1 Pro (High) to Claude Opus 4.6 (Thinking). No need to comment on this change if the user doesn't ask about it. If reporting what model you are, please use a human readable name instead of the exact string.\n</USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>"}
