{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T14:27:20Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nInvestigations for Musculoskeletal Disorders BPT 3rd Year Physiotherapy | Easy-language university exam notes | 10-mark answers Reference: David J. Magee, Orthopedic Physical Assessment (assessment principles). Simplified study notes; not verbatim textbook content. Contents • 1. Blood Investigations and Markers • 2. Ultrasonography (USG) • 3. X-ray (Radiography) • 4. Computed Tomography (CT Scan) • 5. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) Page 1 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes1. Blood Investigations and Markers Definition: Blood investigations are laboratory tests performed on a blood sample to detect inflammation, infection, autoimmune disease, muscle injury, and metabolic problems affecting bones and joints. In simple words: Blood tests help identify whether a musculoskeletal problem may be due to infection, inflammation, an immune disorder, or a metabolic problem. Aims • Detect infection and inflammation. • Identify autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis. • Assess bone and mineral metabolism. • Detect muscle damage. • Support diagnosis and differential diagnosis. • Monitor disease activity and response to treatment. Important blood investigations • CBC (Complete Blood Count): Measures red cells, white cells, hemoglobin, and platelets. Increased WBCs may suggest infection; low hemoglobin may indicate anemia; platelets may rise during inflammation. • ESR (Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate): Measures how far red blood cells settle in one hour. It may rise in inflammatory disorders and infections. It is a nonspecific marker and cannot identify the exact disease. • CRP (C-Reactive Protein): A protein produced mainly by the liver during inflammation. It rises in infection and inflammatory disorders and is useful for monitoring treatment response. It usually changes faster than ESR. • RF (Rheumatoid Factor): An autoantibody that may be present in rheumatoid arthritis. It supports diagnosis but can also be positive in other conditions or some healthy older adults. • Anti-CCP: An antibody against citrullinated proteins. It is relatively specific for rheumatoid arthritis and can help with early diagnosis. • Serum uric acid: Measures urate in blood. It may be elevated in gout, but a high result alone does not confirm gout; joint-fluid crystal analysis may be needed. • CK (Creatine Kinase): An enzyme found mainly in skeletal muscle. It may increase with muscle damage, muscular dystrophy, inflammatory myopathy, or severe muscle injury. • ALP (Alkaline Phosphatase): An enzyme found in bone and liver. It may rise with increased bone turnover, Paget disease, some metabolic bone disorders, or fracture healing. • Calcium, phosphate, and vitamin D: Help assess bone mineral metabolism. Vitamin D supports calcium absorption; these tests may be used when metabolic bone disease or deficiency is suspected. • HLA-B27: A genetic marker associated with ankylosing spondylitis and related disorders. It supports diagnosis when symptoms fit but does not confirm disease by itself. Other useful tests • ANA and anti-dsDNA when systemic autoimmune connective-tissue disease is suspected. • Renal function tests to assess kidney function and help guide medication choices. • PTH when calcium or phosphate abnormalities require investigation. • Blood cultures when bloodstream infection or bone/joint infection is suspected. Examples: RA—RF, anti-CCP, ESR, CRP; osteomyelitis—CBC, ESR, CRP, cultures; gout—urate plus joint-fluid analysis; suspected muscle disease—CK; metabolic bone disease—calcium, phosphate, ALP, vitamin D, and sometimes PTH. Page 2 BPT Physiotherapy Study NotesPhysiotherapy importance: Results help recognize systemic illness, guide referral and precautions, and support rehabilitation planning. Interpret them with history, examination, and imaging. Conclusion: Blood tests support the diagnosis of musculoskeletal disorders but usually cannot establish the diagnosis alone. Page 3 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes2. Ultrasonography (USG) Definition: USG is an imaging technique that uses high-frequency sound waves to create images of muscles, tendons, ligaments, joints, and other soft tissues. In simple words: It uses sound waves to look inside the body without radiation. Principle • A transducer sends sound waves into tissues. • Different tissues reflect the waves differently. • The transducer receives returning echoes. • A computer converts the echoes into an image. Procedure • Position the patient to expose the area. • Apply gel to the skin. • Move the transducer over the area in different directions. • The examiner may ask the patient to move to observe structures dynamically. Indications • Rotator cuff and other tendon injuries. • Muscle strains, tears, and hematomas. • Bursitis and tenosynovitis. • Joint effusion. • Ganglion cysts and selected peripheral nerve problems. • Guidance for selected injections or aspirations. Possible findings • Tendon thickening or tear. • Disruption of muscle fibers. • Fluid around a joint. • Bursal swelling. • Abnormal tendon movement. Advantages • No ionizing radiation. • Usually painless and non-invasive. • Real-time and dynamic assessment. • Can compare both sides. • Relatively inexpensive and portable. • Can guide selected procedures. Limitations • Operator-dependent. • Deep structures may be difficult to assess. Page 4 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes• Bone blocks sound waves. • Cannot adequately assess bone marrow. • Not suitable for every internal joint structure. Physiotherapy importance: Supports assessment of soft-tissue injuries, helps with rehabilitation planning, and may help monitor selected changes. It complements rather than replaces clinical examination. Conclusion: USG is especially useful for superficial soft tissues and movement-related assessment. Page 5 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes3. X-ray (Radiography) Definition: X-ray is an imaging technique using ionizing radiation to produce images of bones and joints. In simple words: It is mainly used to see bones, fractures, and joint alignment. Principle • X-rays pass through the body and are absorbed differently by different tissues. • Dense bone absorbs more radiation and appears white; soft tissues appear gray and air appears black. Procedure • Position the body part. • Place it between the X-ray source and detector. • Take the required view, often two views at right angles. • Assess bone alignment, joint spaces, and abnormalities. Common views • AP (anteroposterior), lateral, and oblique views. • Weight-bearing views when clinically indicated. Indications and findings • Fracture: Site, pattern, displacement, and alignment. • Dislocation: Abnormal position of bones at a joint. • Osteoarthritis: Joint-space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and sometimes cysts. • Rheumatoid arthritis: Possible erosions, joint-space narrowing, and periarticular osteopenia. • Other: Spinal curvature, later changes of osteomyelitis, and some bone lesions. Advantages • Quick, widely available, and relatively inexpensive. • Good for bone, fractures, dislocations, and alignment. • Useful for monitoring fracture healing. Limitations • Uses ionizing radiation. • Produces two-dimensional images. • Limited soft-tissue detail. • Early disease or subtle fractures may not be visible. • Does not directly show cartilage, ligaments, or tendons well. Physiotherapy importance: Helps identify structural problems, understand alignment and healing, and follow medical precautions for loading and exercise. Conclusion: X-ray is commonly a first-line investigation for suspected bone and joint disorders. Page 6 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes4. Computed Tomography (CT Scan) Definition: CT is an imaging technique that uses X-rays and computer processing to produce detailed cross-sectional images, with optional 3D reconstructions. In simple words: It combines X-ray information from many angles to show detailed slices of the body. Principle • An X-ray tube rotates around the patient. • Measurements are collected from different angles. • A computer reconstructs cross-sectional images and may create 3D views. Procedure • The patient lies on a motorized table. • The affected part is positioned in the scanner. • The scanner collects X-ray data while rotating. • A computer produces images in different planes. Indications • Complex, comminuted, and intra-articular fractures. • Pelvic, acetabular, calcaneal, and spinal fractures. • Detailed assessment of vertebral and other bony structures. • Bone destruction, abnormal bone formation, and selected tumors. • Preoperative planning and selected image-guided procedures. Advantages • Detailed cross-sectional bone images. • Useful for complex fractures and fracture displacement. • Allows 3D reconstruction. • Helpful for preoperative planning. Limitations • Uses ionizing radiation, generally more than plain X-ray. • Soft-tissue contrast is usually poorer than MRI. • More expensive than X-ray. • Metal implants can cause artifacts. • Some examinations require contrast material. Physiotherapy importance: Helps understand complex fracture patterns and bony involvement, supporting postoperative rehabilitation planning and precautions. Conclusion: CT is particularly useful for complex fractures and detailed bony anatomy. Page 7 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes5. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) Definition: MRI uses a strong magnetic field, radiofrequency waves, and computer processing to produce detailed images of body tissues. In simple words: MRI is especially useful for muscles, ligaments, tendons, cartilage, bone marrow, and spinal structures. Principle • Hydrogen nuclei respond to a strong magnetic field. • Radiofrequency pulses change their alignment temporarily. • As they return, they produce signals. • A computer converts these signals into detailed images. Procedure • Screen the patient for MRI safety, including implants and metallic objects. • Position the patient on the MRI table. • Acquire images in different planes and sequences. • Use contrast material only when indicated. Indications • Ligaments: ACL, PCL, collateral ligament, and selected shoulder injuries. • Tendons and muscles: Rotator cuff or Achilles tears, muscle tears, hematomas, and tendinopathy. • Joints: Meniscal tears, cartilage injury, effusion, synovitis, and osteonecrosis. • Spine: Disc herniation, spinal stenosis, nerve-root compression, spinal cord abnormalities, and infection. • Tumors: Extent of bone marrow and surrounding soft-tissue involvement. Possible findings • Ligament or tendon tears. • Muscle injury and cartilage damage. • Bone marrow edema. • Disc herniation or nerve compression. • Soft-tissue masses. Advantages • No ionizing radiation. • Excellent soft-tissue contrast. • Shows bone marrow abnormalities. • Useful for ligaments, tendons, cartilage, and spinal structures. • Images can be obtained in multiple planes. Limitations • Expensive and often takes longer than X-ray or CT. • Claustrophobia may be a problem. • Some implants or metallic foreign bodies need special safety assessment. Page 8 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes• Movement can reduce image quality. • Some abnormalities also occur in people without symptoms. Physiotherapy importance: Supports assessment of soft-tissue and spinal conditions, helps plan rehabilitation precautions, and may guide referral. MRI findings must be matched with symptoms and examination. Conclusion: MRI is valuable for soft-tissue, bone marrow, and spinal disorders and does not use ionizing radiation.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-17T19:57:20+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\n<USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>\nThe user changed setting `Model Selection` from Claude Opus 4.6 (Thinking) to Gemini 3.1 Pro (High). No need to comment on this change if the user doesn't ask about it. If reporting what model you are, please use a human readable name instead of the exact string.\n</USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>"}
{"step_index":6,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T14:28:25Z","content":"**Investigations for Musculoskeletal Disorders** BPT 3rd Year Physiotherapy Easy language university exam notes 10 mark answers Reference David J Magee Orthopedic Physical Assessment assessment principles Simplified study notes not verbatim textbook content **Contents** • 1 Blood Investigations and Markers • 2 Ultrasonography USG • 3 X ray Radiography • 4 Computed Tomography CT Scan • 5 Magnetic Resonance Imaging MRI --- **1 Blood Investigations and Markers** **Definition** Blood investigations are laboratory tests performed on a blood sample to detect inflammation infection autoimmune disease muscle injury and metabolic problems affecting bones and joints In simple words Blood tests help identify whether a musculoskeletal problem may be due to infection inflammation an immune disorder or a metabolic problem **Aims** • Detect infection and inflammation • Identify autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis • Assess bone and mineral metabolism • Detect muscle damage • Support diagnosis and differential diagnosis • Monitor disease activity and response to treatment **Important blood investigations** • CBC Complete Blood Count Measures red cells white cells hemoglobin and platelets Increased WBCs may suggest infection low hemoglobin may indicate anemia platelets may rise during inflammation • ESR Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate Measures how far red blood cells settle in one hour It may rise in inflammatory disorders and infections It is a nonspecific marker and cannot identify the exact disease • CRP C Reactive Protein A protein produced mainly by the liver during inflammation It rises in infection and inflammatory disorders and is useful for monitoring treatment response It usually changes faster than ESR • RF Rheumatoid Factor An autoantibody that may be present in rheumatoid arthritis It supports diagnosis but can also be positive in other conditions or some healthy older adults • Anti CCP An antibody against citrullinated proteins It is relatively specific for rheumatoid arthritis and can help with early diagnosis • Serum uric acid Measures urate in blood It may be elevated in gout but a high result alone does not confirm gout joint fluid crystal analysis may be needed • CK Creatine Kinase An enzyme found mainly in skeletal muscle It may increase with muscle damage muscular dystrophy inflammatory myopathy or severe muscle injury • ALP Alkaline Phosphatase An enzyme found in bone and liver It may rise with increased bone turnover Paget disease some metabolic bone disorders or fracture healing • Calcium phosphate and vitamin D Help assess bone mineral metabolism Vitamin D supports calcium absorption these tests may be used when metabolic bone disease or deficiency is suspected • HLA B27 A genetic marker associated with ankylosing spondylitis and related disorders It supports diagnosis when symptoms fit but does not confirm disease by itself **Other useful tests** • ANA and anti dsDNA when systemic autoimmune connective tissue disease is suspected • Renal function tests to assess kidney function and help guide medication choices • PTH when calcium or phosphate abnormalities require investigation • Blood cultures when bloodstream infection or bone/joint infection is suspected **Examples** RA RF anti CCP ESR CRP osteomyelitis CBC ESR CRP cultures gout urate plus joint fluid analysis suspected muscle disease CK metabolic bone disease calcium phosphate ALP vitamin D and sometimes PTH **Physiotherapy importance** Results help recognize systemic illness guide referral and precautions and support rehabilitation planning Interpret them with history examination and imaging **Conclusion** Blood tests support the diagnosis of musculoskeletal disorders but usually cannot establish the diagnosis alone --- **2 Ultrasonography USG** **Definition** USG is an imaging technique that uses high frequency sound waves to create images of muscles tendons ligaments joints and other soft tissues In simple words It uses sound waves to look inside the body without radiation **Principle** • A transducer sends sound waves into tissues • Different tissues reflect the waves differently • The transducer receives returning echoes • A computer converts the echoes into an image **Procedure** • Position the patient to expose the area • Apply gel to the skin • Move the transducer over the